'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>A common claim about extra virgin olive oil is that it <strong>“loses all its benefits” as soon as it is heated</strong>. That is not accurate. Heat does change EVOO: some aromatic and phenolic compounds decline, especially as temperature and heating time rise. But the oil does not suddenly become nutritionally empty or equivalent to every refined cooking oil.</p>
<p>It helps to separate three different questions: <strong>fat stability, minor bioactive compounds and flavour</strong>. They do not respond to heat in exactly the same way.</p>
<h2>What changes when EVOO is heated?</h2>
<p>Extra virgin olive oil consists mainly of triglycerides with a high proportion of oleic acid, plus a much smaller fraction of compounds responsible for much of its aroma, bitterness, pungency and antioxidant activity. This fraction includes several phenolic compounds.</p>
<p>As temperature increases, some of those minor compounds degrade or transform. Domestic cooking studies have measured substantial reductions in phenolics at higher temperatures. This supports a sensible conclusion: <strong>using EVOO raw preserves more of its original phenolic profile</strong>. It does not support the idea that a few minutes in a pan destroys everything valuable in the oil.</p>
<h2>Temperature and time both matter</h2>
<p>A quick sauté, medium-temperature cooking, baking and long deep-frying are not the same treatment. Neither is heating fresh oil once compared with subjecting it to repeated heating and cooling cycles.</p>
<p>More severe heating generally means a greater loss of fresh aromas and some phenolic compounds. If your main goal is to experience the character of an early-harvest EVOO, it therefore makes sense to reserve some for finishing the dish raw.</p>
<h2>Is extra virgin olive oil stable for cooking?</h2>
<p>Yes, when used sensibly. Olive oil is rich in monounsaturated fat and has good heat stability compared with oils that are very rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids. The International Olive Council gives a smoke point of roughly 210&nbsp;°C for olive oil, above normal frying temperatures.</p>
<p>That is not an invitation to heat oil until it smokes. <strong>Visible smoke means the oil is being overheated</strong> and degradation is accelerating. Good cooking is about controlling temperature, not testing the maximum heat an oil can tolerate.</p>
<h2>Do the antioxidants disappear?</h2>
<p>Some decrease, but phenolics are a family of different compounds and they do not all respond identically. Certain molecules are more heat-sensitive while others are relatively more stable. Oleocanthal, for example, can retain some stability during heating when initially present at meaningful levels, even though the broader phenolic profile changes.</p>
<p>The practical conclusion is that <strong>raw EVOO and cooked EVOO do not have exactly the same chemical profile</strong>, yet cooking with EVOO remains entirely compatible with an olive-oil-based diet.</p>
<h2>What about vitamin E?</h2>
<p>Tocopherols, including vitamin E, can also decline during intense or repeated heating. Again, there is no instant switch that removes them all. The degree of change depends on the oil, temperature, duration and repeated use.</p>
<h2>What happens to flavour?</h2>
<p>Heat softens some of the green and fruity aromas that distinguish fresh EVOO. A very expressive oil may therefore taste milder after cooking. When fruitiness, bitterness and pungency are central to a dish, adding a small amount at the end preserves them better.</p>
<p>That does not make cooking with good EVOO a waste. Its fatty-acid composition still matters and some flavour becomes part of the food.</p>
<h2>Can you fry with EVOO?</h2>
<p>Yes. Olive oil's stability makes it suitable for frying when temperature is controlled and the oil is not burned. The main problems are <strong>overheating, burnt food debris and excessive repeated reuse</strong>, not the fact that the oil is extra virgin.</p>
<p>If your oil develops foam, read <a href="/en/why-oil-foams-when-frying-causes-when-to-worry/">why oil foams during frying</a>. For circulating-air cooking, see our guide to <a href="/en/can-you-use-evoo-in-air-fryer-temperature-amount-how-to-apply/">using EVOO in an air fryer</a>.</p>
<h2>How to get more from a good EVOO</h2>
<ul>
<li>Use enough heat to cook, but avoid visible smoking.</li>
<li>Do not leave an almost empty pan of oil over maximum heat for long periods.</li>
<li>Remove burnt crumbs if frying oil is going to be reused.</li>
<li>Protect the bottle from light, heat and air during storage.</li>
<li>When aroma matters, keep a little oil for a raw finishing drizzle.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Is a good EVOO worth using for cooking?</h2>
<p>Yes. Saying that “good EVOO is only for raw use” is too simplistic. Raw use displays its full personality and protects heat-sensitive compounds better, but its composition also makes it a useful cooking fat. A highly aromatic premium oil can be reserved for finishing, while another balanced EVOO can serve as an everyday oil for pans, ovens and stews.</p>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p><strong>Heating extra virgin olive oil changes some of its properties but does not erase them all.</strong> Aromas and part of its phenolic content decline as heat exposure increases, while olive oil retains useful cooking stability. The smartest approach is not “raw only” versus “cooking only”, but choosing the use that fits the dish.</p>
